  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nStill some stronger storms working their way through the area \nwith some 40-50+ mph gusts, but the severe weather threat is \ngenerally waning as outflow continues to surge southeastward \nahead of the line. Storms should exit the area by 3-4 AM with \nguidance and satellite imagery hinting that we see a little \npatchy fog and low cloud development behind them as the anvil \nclears out into Friday morning. We'll remain under cyclonic flow\naloft and on the back side of a surface low over IA through the\nday, with guidance suggesting potential for a few spotty \nshowers, but nothing too impactful from that standpoint. Perhaps\nmore noteworthy will be the strong northerly winds. Model \nsoundings suggest at least 40+ kts at the top of the mixed \nlayer. In addition, EPS guidance shows mean wind gusts of 40-50 \nmph with a 10-20% chance of exceeding 50 mph. Really it will \ncome down to how deep we're able to mix, and given the expected \ncloud cover and precip, lean toward shallower mixing and \nslightly lighter winds, but still expect 40+ mph gusts at times.\nCurrent forecast has a few counties sitting right around Wind \nAdvisory criteria, but given lower confidence in degree of \nmixing, going to hold off for now. Otherwise, we'll be much \ncooler with highs in the mid 70s to lower 80s.\n\nSaturday will remain mild with dry weather expected as the \ntrough/low push farther east and surface high pressure builds \nin. We'll start to warm back up Sunday with strengthening \nsoutherly flow on the back side of the high and upper level \nridging building back in. Expect highs in the 80s to around 90 \non Sunday, followed by mid 80s to mid 90s on Monday.\n\nBy Monday night into Tuesday, we'll start to see influence of a\nlarger scale trough to our north while a cold front starts \nedging southward. Still a decent amount of spread on exact \ntiming of when that front gets here, but when it does, it'll \nbring our next chance of showers and storms. Guidance also \nsuggests it stalls over or just south of the forecast area, \nwhile several bits of shortwave energy slide through through the\nend of the week. So in general, expect a somewhat active week \nwith on and off shower and storm chances with temperatures in \nthe 80s in the northern part of the area and 90s in the south. \nMachine learning severe weather guidance also suggests at least \nsome low end severe storm chances at times (5-10%), but still \nlots of details to work out between now and then.",
